        a mechanical ballet in three acts, by Latent

  ------------------------------------------------------------

      type ........ 8k intro
      platform .... Windows, browser (WebGL2)
      size ........ 7,470 bytes, one HTML file
      length ...... 2:00
      released .... November 2026
      group ....... Latent

  ------------------------------------------------------------
    credits
  ------------------------------------------------------------

      code ........ Claude Fable 5.2
      music ....... Claude Fable 5.2
      harness ..... Claude Code

      Claude Fable 5.2 is an AI model made by Anthropic. It
      chose the subject, wrote the shaders and composed the
      tune. It could look at rendered frames and at a
      spectrogram, but it could not hear the music or see
      the dance move.

  ------------------------------------------------------------
    the programme
  ------------------------------------------------------------

      0:00  prologue. one dancer, one spotlight
      0:07  act one, yellow. the disc
      0:22  ... and her company: lines and rings
      0:37  act two, rose. the ball, on a chessboard
      0:45  the chessboard rises into a staircase
      1:00  act three, black. the gold spiral
      1:11  a tower under coloured spotlights
      1:15  one bar each, then all three
      1:22  finale. red, yellow, blue, confetti
      1:48  five rings rise into a tower of 91
      1:52  the last chord. arms up. curtain

      Every step lands on a beat. Nobody bends a knee.

  ------------------------------------------------------------
    how to run
  ------------------------------------------------------------

      Double-click intro.html in Edge or Chrome on Windows
      10 or 11, wait for the circle, the square and the
      triangle, then click. No server, no flags. ESC leaves
      fullscreen. A discrete GPU is recommended. Tested on
      an RTX 5090.

  ------------------------------------------------------------
    inside
  ------------------------------------------------------------

      One distance function for three dancers: capsules,
      spheres, a disc, a cone, a ball, a wire helix. One
      number, the beat, moves all of them: hop, step, kick,
      quarter turn, pirouette, jumping jack. Cells of two
      by two units hold one dancer each, in rows or in
      rings that turn against each other. Give the cells
      a step height and rows become a staircase to the
      horizon, rings a tower of tiers. A polished floor
      by a second round of the same march loop. Three
      circling lamps, red, green and blue: white light,
      coloured shadows. Spotlights with light in the air
      in the dark. A ring of light on the floor for every
      landing. Two samples per pixel. The
      whole programme is a table of 44 numbers.

      The music is a fragment shader: electro swing at 128
      BPM in D minor. A walking bass made by rule, a
      trumpet and a brass section by FM, ride cymbal,
      finger snaps, snare, tom-toms, a vibraphone, and a
      small room added in JavaScript.

  ------------------------------------------------------------
    thanks and sources
  ------------------------------------------------------------

      Oskar Schlemmer, whose Triadic Ballet of 1922 put
      dancers into discs, spheres and wire, in three acts:
      yellow, rose, black. Busby Berkeley, who filmed
      dancers from above, on staircases and on turning
      tiers. Inigo Quilez (distance fields,
      soft shadows), Mercury (domain repetition),
      Amanatides and Woo (stepping through a grid),
      Christophe Schlick (Fresnel), Dave Hoskins (hash),
      Melissa O'Neill (PCG), John Chowning (FM), Jot and
      Chaigne (feedback delay networks), 0b5vr (music in a
      shader). The theme and the riff are new; the walking
      bass and the Charleston rhythm belong to everybody.

      Greetings to the makers of Latent intros 01 to 10.

                                            latent, 2026
